{
 "schema": 1,
 "verified": true,
 "edition": "IEC 60664-1:2020-05",
 "what_this_is": "The tabulated spacing data of IEC 60664-1:2020-05, recovered from the KiCad 10.0 calculator implementation and reproduced exactly by calc/iec60664.py. Every ladder is a list of [upper_input, value]: value applies when the input is <= upper_input (the standard's own '<=' semantics). A value written as {\"slope\": a, \"intercept\": b} means the gap is not tabulated in that range but computed as a*input+b - see formula_steps.",
 "how_it_was_obtained": "pcb_calculator/calculator_panels/iec60664.cpp from KiCad 10.0 was compiled unmodified (only a missing <cstdlib> include was added) and probed. Breakpoints were recovered by bisection against the compiled code; values are re-read from the raw ladder functions (GetBasicCreepageDistance / GetClearanceToWithstandTransientVoltage / GetClearanceToWithstandPeaks) so the 'creepage may not be smaller than clearance' clamp cannot hide a table step. The port is then diffed against the compiled code on the preferred-voltage grid (3760 points), a dense geometric sweep (91k points) and this raw-ladder probe (58k points): zero mismatches. The single non-constant segment (PD2 x IIIa/IIIb, V/100 above 160 V) is transcribed from source line 1057 and covered by the same diffs.",
 "sources": [
